                <pb facs="0834" n="734" sameAs="frus:frus1920v03/pg734"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">715.16/23: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Salvador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Arnold</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >San Salvador</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-05-17T11:00:00-05:56"><hi rendition="#italic">May 17, 1920—11
                                a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received May 18—9:12 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>19. Department’s 10, May 15, 5 p.m. President emphatically denies the reports. No
                    ultimatum has been sent to Honduras and no invasion of Hondurean territory has
                    taken place. Honduras has promised to send Arturo Araujo and chief
                    revolutionists out of the country. Until they have left Honduras, troops will be
                    kept on Salvadorean frontier as precautionary measure. Doctor Salvador Corleto
                    special agent of Honduras is at present in San Salvador and the President said
                    all questions will be settled amicably between the two countries.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Arnold</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
